# Committed readings for fs-descriptor.wasm: wasi:filesystem@0.3's preopen, descriptor, open-at, stat, stat-at, get-flags,
# get-type, read-via-stream and write-via-stream at the ABI (#1374 E1a, ADR 0133). Default path for each Go row: gate:async
# on, gate:cm-threads both off and on.
# Built:       wasm-tools parse fs-descriptor.wat -o fs-descriptor.wasm   (wasm-tools 1.258.0; validates --features all)
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run -W component-model-async=y -W component-model-more-async-builtins=y \
#                -S p3=y --dir <tree>::/ --invoke 'run(<row>)' fs-descriptor.wasm
# Tree:        made fresh before every run: hello.txt "hello world", w.txt "0123456789", sub/inner.txt "inner",
#              in-link -> hello.txt, out-link -> /etc. No row reads a directory, so host directory order is never read.
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64. Runs: 10 of 10 per line, each identical. Taken
#              2026-11-24.
#
# Each line is `<row>: <reading>`, and the test reads them by row. The row's number is its line's position, from 0.
preopens: 19857665
open-read: 110592
content: 1819043176
offset: 1684828783
stat: 184877058
stat-at: 184877058
stat-at-kinds: 67109376
stat-fields: 16843009
get-flags: 289
get-type: 328194
write-at: 302051328
write-content: 1093808432
write-read-only-fd: 8519936
escapes: 2661195422
symlinks: 262286
in-grant: 184549376
errors: 2543032211
nested: 43909120
sync-lower: 184877056
create: 33562624
#
# The two read-only rows come from the WIT's text, not from wasmtime: wasmtime 49.0.3's `--dir` always grants
# read|mutate-directory (Burroughs' `--scratch`), so its CLI has no read-only grant to read them on. Burroughs runs them
# on a read-only preopen and the test asserts these values. open-at's text: "If `flags` contains `write` or
# `mutate-directory`, or `open-flags` contains `truncate` or `create`, and the base descriptor doesn't have
# `descriptor-flags::mutate-directory` set, `open-at` fails with `error-code::read-only`."
read-only-opens: 2694881281
read-only-truncate: 721056
#
# Meaning: four bytes, byte 0 the low one. An operation's byte is 0 for ok and 0x80 | its error-code case for an error
# (0x82 bad-descriptor, 0x8e loop, 0x93 no-entry, 0x97 not-directory, 0x9e not-permitted, 0xa0 read-only). A stream
# copy's byte is its payload, progress << 4 | COMPLETED (0) or DROPPED (1).
#   preopens           0x012f0101: one preopen; its name is 1 byte, "/"; its handle is nonzero.
#   open-read          0x0001b000: open-at hello.txt ok; the first read COMPLETED with 11; the second DROPPED with 0; the
#                      future ok.
#   content            0x6c6c6568: "hell", the first four bytes read.
#   offset             0x646c726f: "orld", read-via-stream(7) to the end.
#   stat               0x0b050002: the async lower RETURNED (2) inline; ok; regular-file (5); size 11.
#   stat-at            the same, through stat-at(symlink-follow, hello.txt) on the grant.
#   stat-at-kinds      0x04000200: stat-at sub ok, directory (2); stat-at in-link unfollowed ok, symbolic-link (4).
#   stat-fields        0x01010101: hello.txt's link-count 1; its access, modification and status-change times all some.
#   get-flags          0x00000121: the grant reads 33 (read|mutate-directory); hello.txt opened read reads 1.
#   get-type           0x00050202: the async lower RETURNED; the grant is a directory (2); hello.txt a regular-file (5).
#   write-at           0x1200f000: w.txt opened read|write ok; write-via-stream(3)'s write COMPLETED with 15; the future
#                      ok; 18 bytes read back. The host's w.txt reads "012ABCDEFGHIJKLMNO" after the run.
#   write-content      0x41323130: "012A", the read-back's first four bytes.
#   write-read-only-fd 0x00820100: hello.txt opened read ok; the write DROPPED with 0; the future err(bad-descriptor).
#                      hello.txt is unchanged after the run.
#   escapes            0x9e9e9e9e: open-at ../x, /etc, sub/../../x and out-link followed are each not-permitted.
#   symlinks           0x0004008e: open-at out-link unfollowed is loop; stat-at out-link unfollowed ok, symbolic-link;
#                      open-at in-link followed ok.
#   in-grant           0x0b000000: open-at sub/../hello.txt ok; sub/inner.txt ok; stat-at in-link followed ok, size 11.
#   errors             0x97939793: open-at missing.txt no-entry; hello.txt/x not-directory; stat-at missing.txt no-entry;
#                      open-at hello.txt with open-flags directory not-directory.
#   nested             0x029e0000: open-at sub with open-flags directory ok; inner.txt from it ok; ../hello.txt from it
#                      not-permitted, since the base is sub; sub is a directory.
#   sync-lower         0x0b050000: open-at and stat through their sync lowers: ok, ok, regular-file, size 11.
#   create             0x02002000: new.txt opened create|truncate, read|write ok; a write COMPLETED with 2; the future
#                      ok; its size 2. The host's new.txt reads "AB" after the run.
#   read-only-opens    0xa0a0a001: on a read-only grant, the grant's flags are read (1); open-at hello.txt with write,
#                      new.txt with create, and sub with mutate-directory are each read-only. On wasmtime's
#                      read|mutate-directory grant the row reads 33: flags 33 and three oks, and new.txt is created.
#   read-only-truncate 0x000b00a0: on a read-only grant, open-at hello.txt with truncate is read-only; hello.txt read to its
#                      end has its future ok and 11 bytes, so nothing was truncated. On wasmtime's grant the row reads 0:
#                      the truncate is ok, and 0 bytes are read back.
